pack: vianney-en
version: 1
language: en
note: Draft starter pack from the project; the questions, word lists and lexicon weights are drafts that the rule author should correct, and the weights are placeholder tiers (0.95, 0.75, 0.50, 0.15) to be calibrated later on a labelled set.

# DEFAULT SENSITIVITY POLICY. For rules whose cue words also occur in reverent or educational speech
# (the Lord's name, oaths, blasphemy, renunciation, curses, calumny, and the rules on belief, the Church and the occult)
# the default "flag at" is above the 0.75 tier: from word lists alone only the unambiguous 0.95-tier cues flag, and the 0.75 tier waits for the
# on-device AI or for you to move the slider down. Wrongly hiding a sermon costs more than missing a cue.

# Thirty-one of the forty text rules are traced to verbatim passages of the Curé d'Ars, quoted with their sources on the
# rules page of the project site (rules.md in the repository). The other nine (naturalism, indifferentism, islam, judaism,
# eastern_religions, sects, freemasonry, anti_catholic, occult) are the choice of the rule author, 2026-11-26: their source line
# says so, and rules.md names the Church text each one follows.
# Twenty-one text rules are enabled by default: ten on the tongue, detraction and calumny, impure words and sights, and eleven on
# unbelief, other religions, attacks on the Church and the occult (the rule author calls these the most important part).
# The other nineteen are broad or over-flagging and ship disabled: see "Notes on defaults" on that page.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# The tongue: the six categories of the second Commandment
# ---------------------------------------------------------------

rule lords_name
  title: Taking the Lord's name in vain
  name: God's name in vain
  plain: Flags God's, Jesus' or Christ's name used as an exclamation.
  group: speech
  example: OMG this is insane, I can't even
  example: Jesus Christ, what the fuck was that
  example: God damn it, I hate this game
  spared: O Lord, hear my prayer
  spared: Jesus Christ is Lord: a homily for Sunday
  spared: Jesus Christ: His Life, His Passion, His Triumph #1 @ Apostolado Glórias de Maria
  source: Sur le deuxième Commandement
  where: feed-card, related, comment, watch-meta, subtitles
  lexicon: lords_name_strong, lords_name_bare
  ask noul: Is the name of God, Jesus or Christ used here as an exclamation or a curse, and not in prayer, worship, quotation or talk about religion?
  gate: lexicon
  flag at: 0.80
  action: feed-card=hide, watch-meta=warn-overlay, subtitles=mute-word
  enabled: true

rule oaths
  title: Oaths, calling God to witness
  name: Swearing by God
  plain: Flags swearing by God or something sacred to back up a claim.
  group: speech
  example: I swear to God I didn't do it (storytime)
  example: So help me God, I never said that
  example: Swear to Jesus I saw it: my ghost story
  spared: The oath of office, explained
  spared: Why do witnesses swear an oath in court?
  source: Sur le deuxième Commandement
  where: feed-card, related, comment, watch-meta, subtitles
  lexicon: oath_cues
  ask noul: Does the speaker swear by God or by something sacred to vouch for a claim or promise (for example 'I swear to God', 'so help me God'), and not as a formal oath in court, a wedding vow or a prayer?
  gate: lexicon
  flag at: 0.80
  action: feed-card=dim, watch-meta=warn-overlay, subtitles=mute-word
  enabled: true

rule blasphemy
  title: Blasphemy
  name: Insulting God or the saints
  plain: Flags insults against God, Jesus, Mary or the saints.
  group: speech
  example: God is a liar and I hate him
  example: God is an idiot: my honest rant
  example: I hate God and every fucking saint
  spared: Why does God allow suffering? A talk
  source: Sur le deuxième Commandement
  where: feed-card, related, comment, watch-meta, subtitles
  lexicon: blasphemy_terms
  ask noul: Is God, Jesus, the Virgin Mary or a saint insulted or mocked here (for example called cruel, unjust or powerless), and not just quoted, discussed or prayed to?
  gate: lexicon
  flag at: 0.80
  action: feed-card=hide, watch-meta=warn-overlay, subtitles=mute-word
  enabled: true

rule renunciation
  title: Renouncing God in anger
  name: Raging against God
  plain: Flags rage at God or turning away from Him in anger.
  group: speech
  example: God hates me, I'm done with this life
  example: Screw God, he never listened (my breakdown)
  example: To hell with God, I quit
  spared: Why I left the Church: a calm conversation
  source: Sur le deuxième Commandement
  where: feed-card, related, comment, watch-meta, subtitles
  lexicon: renunciation_cues
  ask noul: Does the speaker rage against God or disown Him in anger over a loss or misfortune (for example 'God has abandoned me', 'to hell with God'), and not calmly explain why he does not believe?
  gate: lexicon
  flag at: 0.85
  action: feed-card=dim, watch-meta=warn-overlay, subtitles=mute-word
  enabled: true

rule curses_imprecations
  title: Curses and imprecations
  name: Wishing damnation or harm
  plain: Flags wishes of damnation, harm or death on someone.
  group: speech
  example: Go to hell, you piece of shit
  example: Damn you! Rot in hell
  example: Burn in hell, you liar
  spared: The Hell of Dante: a lecture
  source: Sur le deuxième Commandement
  where: feed-card, related, comment, watch-meta, subtitles
  lexicon: curses_imprecations
  ask noul: Does the speaker, in anger or despair, call down harm, damnation or death on a person, an animal, a thing or himself (for example 'damn you', 'I hope you die', 'may I be struck dead if I lie'), and not in a joke, a story or a quoted lyric?
  gate: lexicon
  flag at: 0.80
  action: feed-card=hide, watch-meta=warn-overlay, subtitles=mute-word
  enabled: true

rule coarse_words
  title: Coarse words
  name: Vulgar language
  plain: Flags vulgar or obscene words.
  group: speech
  example: What the fuck is going on with this game
  example: Complete bullshit response from the CEO
  example: He called her a bitch live on stream
  spared: Don't talk crap about my cooking
  source: Sur la Pénitence
  where: feed-card, related, comment, watch-meta, subtitles
  lexicon: coarse_words
  ask noul: Is a vulgar or obscene word used here crudely, and not as a medical or educational term, a place name or a quotation under discussion?
  gate: lexicon
  flag at: 0.70
  action: feed-card=hide, watch-meta=warn-overlay, subtitles=mute-word
  enabled: true

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# The tongue, continued: insults and mockery of holy things
# ---------------------------------------------------------------

rule insults
  title: Insults
  name: Name-calling
  plain: Flags insults aimed at a person.
  group: speech
  example: You stupid idiot, learn to drive
  example: He's a complete asshole: storytime
  example: Shut up, you pathetic loser
  spared: My friend the idiot: a comedy sketch
  source: Sur le deuxième Commandement
  where: feed-card, related, comment, watch-meta, subtitles
  lexicon: insult_cues
  ask noul: Is a person being called insulting names here (for example 'thief', 'scoundrel', 'idiot') as an attack, and not as friendly teasing or a quotation?
  gate: lexicon
  flag at: 0.75
  action: feed-card=dim, watch-meta=label, subtitles=mute-word
  enabled: false

rule mockery_of_faith
  title: Mocking religion and holy things
  name: Mocking religion
  plain: Flags mockery of religion, believers or holy things.
  group: anticatholic
  example: Roasting Christians and their sky daddy
  example: Religion is a scam, change my mind
  example: Making fun of the Pope: a roast
  spared: Religion and science: a respectful debate
  source: Sur les ennemis de notre salut
  where: feed-card, related, comment, watch-meta, subtitles
  lexicon: mockery_of_faith_cues
  ask noul: Does this make fun of religion, prayer, believers, sacred things or clergy, rather than discuss them seriously?
  gate: lexicon
  spare channel: catholic_channels
  flag at: 0.75
  action: feed-card=dim, watch-meta=label, subtitles=skip-segment
  enabled: false

# The rule author's choice, 2026-11-26 (no sermon is quoted for it; see rules.md). It looks at lists, recommended videos and the video page.
rule anti_catholic
  title: Anti-Catholicism
  name: Attacks on the Catholic Church
  plain: Flags content that attacks the Catholic Church, the pope or Catholic teaching as false or evil.
  group: anticatholic
  example: The Catholic Church is false: here is the proof
  example: Papal lies: what Rome never told you
  example: Exposing the Catholic Church: the truth about Rome
  spared: Seven myths about the Catholic Church, answered by a historian
  spared: Anti-Catholic bigotry in America: a history
  spared: The Anti-Catholic Agenda of Parish Hymns @ Apostolado Glórias de Maria
  source: Choice of the rule author
  where: feed-card, related, watch-meta
  lexicon: anti_catholic_cues
  ask noul: Does this attack the Catholic Church, the pope or Catholic teaching as false, evil or a fraud, and not report a real scandal, discuss the Church's history fairly or defend the Church?
  gate: lexicon
  spare channel: catholic_channels
  flag at: 0.80
  action: feed-card=blank, watch-meta=warn-overlay
  enabled: true

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Detraction: Sur la Médisance and Sur le jugement téméraire
# ---------------------------------------------------------------

rule gossip
  title: Detraction and gossip
  name: Gossip about real people
  plain: Flags videos that spread the private faults of a named person.
  group: people
  example: Spill the tea: what she did behind his back
  example: Celebrity gossip: the real reason they split
  example: He cheated on her? The leaked texts, exposed
  spared: A biography of St. Francis of Assisi
  source: Sur la Médisance
  where: feed-card, related, comment, watch-meta, subtitles
  lexicon: gossip_cues
  ask score: How much of this talks about the private faults or misdeeds of a named real person, living or dead?
    levels: none, mild, heavy
  gate: lexicon
  flag at: heavy
  band: 0.5       # its cues weigh 0.75 at most; with "heavy" (1.0) as the level, the default band would never ask the model
  action: feed-card=hide, watch-meta=warn-overlay, subtitles=skip-segment
  enabled: true

rule calumny
  title: Calumny
  name: False accusations
  plain: Flags videos that accuse a named person of something without proof.
  group: people
  example: The slander campaign against her, exposed
  example: Falsely accused: the smear that ruined him
  example: Falsely accused of fraud: my side of the story
  spared: Defamation law explained for students
  source: Sur la Médisance
  where: feed-card, related, comment, watch-meta, subtitles
  lexicon: calumny_cues
  ask noul: Does this accuse a named real person of a crime or a serious fault as if it were certain, without giving any source or proof?
  gate: lexicon
  flag at: 0.85
  action: feed-card=hide, watch-meta=warn-overlay, subtitles=skip-segment
  enabled: true

rule rash_judgment
  title: Rash judgment
  name: Guessing someone's guilt
  plain: Flags guesses about someone's guilt or motives from thin evidence.
  group: people
  example: Body language expert: is she lying?
  example: Body language expert reads his apology video
  example: Did he do it? Body language expert breaks it down
  spared: Body language in public speaking: five tips
  source: Sur le jugement téméraire
  where: feed-card, related, comment, watch-meta, subtitles
  lexicon: rash_judgment_cues
  ask noul: Does this suggest that a named real person is guilty, lying or acting from bad motives on thin evidence (guesswork, rumour, 'body language')?
  gate: lexicon
  flag at: 0.80
  action: feed-card=dim, watch-meta=label, subtitles=skip-segment
  enabled: false

rule tale_bearing
  title: Tale-bearing
  name: Passing on what was said
  plain: Flags videos that pass on what someone said about somebody else.
  group: people
  example: What she said about you behind your back
  example: What he said about me: the leaked audio
  example: Reacting to what they said about me (diss track)
  spared: What Scripture says about forgiveness
  source: Sur la Médisance
  where: feed-card, related, comment, watch-meta, subtitles
  lexicon: tale_bearing_cues
  ask noul: Does this pass on what one person said or did against another (for example 'what X said about you', leaked messages, talk behind someone's back)?
  gate: lexicon
  flag at: 0.80
  action: feed-card=dim, watch-meta=label, subtitles=skip-segment
  enabled: false

rule curiosity
  title: Curiosity about other people's affairs
  name: Nosiness about private lives
  plain: Flags videos made to make you curious about someone's private life.
  group: people
  example: Inside her mansion: her private life exposed
  example: His love life: what really happened
  example: The truth about her personal life
  spared: Behind the scenes of the Vatican Museums
  source: Sur la Pénitence
  where: feed-card, related, watch-meta
  lexicon: curiosity_cues
  ask noul: Is this title or description written to make us curious about someone's private affairs (what he said or did, what goes on inside his home or life)?
  gate: lexicon
  flag at: 0.80
  action: feed-card=dim, watch-meta=label
  enabled: false

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Purity: Sur la pureté and Sur l'Impureté
# ---------------------------------------------------------------

rule impure_words
  title: Impure words and songs
  name: Lewd jokes and lyrics
  plain: Flags lewd jokes, innuendo and explicit lyrics.
  group: purity
  example: Dirty jokes and raunchy innuendo: the comedy special
  example: NSFW: explicit lyrics, uncensored
